{
  "name": "How to Plan Kitchen Outlet Placement for Wheelchair Users",
  "step": [
    {
      "name": "Step 1",
      "text": "Planning kitchen outlet placement for wheelchair users can feel a bit overwhelming, but we can break it down into a few clear steps to make sure it's done right. First off, you need to map out every usable counter segment. This isn't just about the long stretches; think about smaller sections too. Next, place those receptacles strategically so that every qualifying counter run has accessible power. You've really got to think about reach here – can someone in a wheelchair comfortably plug something in without straining or bumping into things? Thirdly, verify that there's clear floor space in front of each outlet location. This clear space needs to be aligned with the fixture, allowing full access without obstructions. Finally, always account for reachable heights and depths, especially concerning counter depth and any potential obstructions. It's much easier to adjust this during the planning and rough-in phases than after everything's been installed. For complex layouts or if you're unsure about specific accessibility standards, bringing in a pro can be a game-changer.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 1
    }
  ],
